        <section id="parallelisation"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Parallelisation</h2>
              <p>Pure functions allow multiple threads working on the same function simultaneously as they do not rely on external variables. This can massively increase the speed at which a program can be executed.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="parallelisation-and-how-is-it-managed" >
          
            <h2>Parallelisation and how is it managed</h2>
              <p>Pure functions are particularly useful when you want to implement parallel processing. Parallel processing is a way of allocating processes that are happening in a program so that they can make the most use of the computational resources available. It differs from sequential (typical) programs in that it allows multiple processes to occur at once.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/uc/uc_u3_s3_p2_image.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>Sequential programming only allows one process to occur at time. If you think of these processes as people queuing to get through security at the airport, a sequential program would only have one security gate open, meaning that only one person could be processed at a time. In contrast, parallel processing allows as many processes to occur at once within the boundaries of the memory that is available. So a parallel airport might have a long queue, but can open all of the security gates in the terminal at once and therefore get through the queue much quicker.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
